{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00973-SCI', 'source_ref': 'REF-00973', 'target_population': 'SCI', 'study_population': '17 people with SCI, C4-T12, AIS A/B/C, MWC >4h/day, 16 men 1 woman, mean age 39.8', 'sample_size': 17, 'match_grade': 'EXACT', 'mismatch_note': 'TWO MISMATCHES AND THE FIRST IS SEVERE. (i) Inclusion criteria require independent community mobility in the MWC INCLUDING ASCENDING AN ACCESS RAMP, so the study structurally SELECTS OUT exactly the people for whom ramps are already impassable; the finding is a lower bound on population burden. (ii) 16 of 17 participants are men, and shoulder anthropometry and strength are sexually dimorphic, so this cannot ground a claim about women wheelchair users.', 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:10', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00973', 'gap_id': None}
{'match_id': 'session_2026-09-02-resea-REF-00974-MOB', 'source_ref': 'REF-00974', 'target_population': 'MOB', 'study_population': 'ten able-bodied participants', 'sample_size': 10, 'match_grade': 'PROXY', 'mismatch_note': 'Stated plainly: NO WHEELCHAIR USER TOOK PART. Able-bodied participants have intact trunk control, no shoulder overuse history, no SCI-related muscle imbalance and no chronic exposure - they lack the very condition the study exists to inform. The absolute forces are indicative only; the RANKING across activities is the transferable part, and even that is a hypothesis for real users.', 'created_at': '2026-09-02 21:10', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-02-research-batch-05-circulation-icf', 'ref_id': 'REF-00974', 'gap_id': None}
